There are two good options depending on whether you want automatic enforcement or on-demand invocation:

**Option A — workspace instructions** (always-on, auto-enforced)
Put it in `.github/copilot-instructions.md` at the project root. The agent reads this on every interaction so the role-sequence rule is always visible, even if the user never explicitly mentions it. Best for a standing governance rule like "every change must follow this process."

```
a-spice-prj/
  .github/
    copilot-instructions.md   ← add the role order here
    skills/
      project-manager/
      requirements-engineer/
      …
```

**Option B — a dedicated workflow skill** (on-demand, invoked by description match)
Add `.github/skills/change-workflow/SKILL.md`. The skill's `description` would say "Use when making or reviewing any change to the product — executes project-manager → requirements-engineer → … → software-qualification-tester in order." The agent loads it automatically when the user's request matches. Best if you want the orchestration packaged alongside the existing role skills and invoked explicitly.

**Recommendation for this project:** Option A (`.github/copilot-instructions.md`) because:
- The rule "for every change, execute these roles in order" is a standing process requirement, not a task-specific workflow; it should always be in context.
- The project has no `copilot-instructions.md` yet, so this would be a clean addition without touching the existing skill files.
- The eight role skills are already in place; the instruction simply tells the agent to invoke them in sequence.

